================================================================================
MODEL OVERVIEW
================================================================================

MODEL: Windspeed (WS) Pair-Level Uncertainty Model
---------------------------------------------------
Target: Signed relative windspeed deviation between cross-prediction and actual
Formula: e_overall = (WS_pred_overall - WS_actual_overall) / WS_actual_overall
Level: Pair (one observation per directional pair, ~28 pairs)
Distribution: Student-t (robust to outliers, nu ~ 14)

Key design choices:
    - Trains on pair-level overall (omnidirectional) error, not sector errors.
      Sector errors (~11% std) partially cancel when aggregated; the EYA-relevant
      quantity is the omnidirectional error (~4% std).
    - Student-t likelihood handles outlier pairs without distorting the model.
    - Signed errors enable +/- confidence intervals for industry communication.
    - HalfNormal priors on gamma: features can only INCREASE sigma, never
      decrease it. This encodes the physical constraint that more complexity,
      more distance, more mismatch -> more uncertainty. Never less.

Model structure:
    e_overall ~ StudentT(nu, mu, sigma)

    log(sigma) = log_sigma0
               + gamma_dist_norm * dist_sat_z
               + gamma_turning   * ew_abs_turning_z
               + gamma_speedup   * ew_abs_log_speedup_z
               + gamma_roughness * ew_abs_rough_speedup_z
               + gamma_k_MM      * ew_k_MM_z
               + gamma_dz_sat    * dz_sat_z
               + gamma_dz_trix   * dz_trix_z

    mu = beta_dz * dz_z    (signed height difference drives bias direction)

Training data: 28 directional pairs (~14 physical pair groups)
Excluded masts: See PROBLEMATIC_MASTS in ws_uncertainty_model_pairlevel.py

================================================================================
MODEL PERFORMANCE
================================================================================

LOO Cross-Validation (site-level holdout):
    Pearson  r   = 0.757
    Spearman rho = 0.652

Full model fit:
    nu = 13.9 (moderate heavy tails, between Gaussian and Cauchy)
    Baseline sigma (all z=0): exp(-3.50) = 3.0%

Feature ablation results (delta_rho = change in Spearman when feature dropped):
    gamma_roughness:   delta_rho = -0.490  <-- CRITICAL, model collapses without it
    gamma_dist_norm:   delta_rho = -0.084  <-- Important
    gamma_k_MM:        delta_rho = -0.072  <-- Moderate
    gamma_turning:     delta_rho = +0.045  <-- Neutral (removing helps slightly)
    gamma_speedup:     delta_rho = +0.022  <-- Neutral
    gamma_dz_sat:      delta_rho = +0.001  <-- Weak (but physically motivated)
    gamma_dz_trix:     delta_rho = +0.010  <-- Weak (but physically motivated)

Note: dz features were recently changed from linear abs_dz + abs_dz*dist_sat
to saturating dz_sat + dz*TRIX interaction. The ablation above is from the
previous formulation and may shift with the new features.

================================================================================
SIGMA DRIVERS - RANKED BY EFFECT SIZE
================================================================================

7 sigma features + 1 mu (bias) feature, all computed at pair level.

Sector-level values are aggregated to pair level via energy-weighted mean
using weight_energy_predicted.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 1: EW |Roughness Speedup| (gamma = 0.361, multiplier = 1.44x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: rough_speedup_WTG_frac, rough_speedup_MM_frac,
                weight_energy_predicted
Formula:
    Per sector: abs_rough = |(rough_speedup_WTG + rough_speedup_MM) / 2|
    Pair level: ew_abs_rough_speedup = SUM(w_energy_norm * abs_rough)

Description:
    Energy-weighted mean of the absolute average roughness speedup across
    sectors. Both positive (roughness acceleration) and negative (roughness
    deceleration) indicate WAsP is applying a large roughness correction,
    which increases uncertainty regardless of sign.

Physical meaning:
    When WAsP applies large roughness corrections at either site, the
    prediction is sensitive to the accuracy of the roughness map.
    Errors in land cover classification (e.g., forest vs grassland)
    propagate proportionally to the roughness speedup magnitude.

    In flat terrain, roughness is the PRIMARY flow driver — larger roughness
    corrections dominate the prediction. In complex terrain, orographic
    effects overshadow roughness, reducing this feature's importance.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.361 +/- 0.148
    Multiplier: 1.44x per +1 SD
    Ablation: CRITICAL (delta_rho = -0.490, model collapses without it)

Author's note:
    By far the most important feature. This was a surprise — roughness is
    often treated as a secondary consideration after terrain complexity.
    The dominance likely reflects that most training pairs are in
    moderate terrain where roughness corrections are the main uncertainty source.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 2: EW Weibull k at MM (gamma = 0.231, multiplier = 1.26x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: k_MM, weight_energy_predicted
Formula:
    Per sector: k_MM_sector (raw Weibull shape parameter at MM site)
    Pair level: ew_k_MM = SUM(w_energy_norm * k_MM_sector)

Description:
    Energy-weighted Weibull shape parameter at the predictor (MM) site.
    Higher k means a more peaked (narrower) wind speed distribution.

Physical meaning:
    Higher k = more peaked wind distribution = energy concentrated in a
    narrower wind speed range = higher sensitivity to directional modelling
    errors. If WAsP gets the direction slightly wrong, more energy shifts
    between sectors.

    Uses MM-site data only — valid for deployment when only the predictor
    mast has measurements.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.231 +/- 0.151
    Multiplier: 1.26x per +1 SD

Note:
    This is the raw energy-weighted k value, NOT a deviation from 2 or
    from the pair mean. The hypothesis is that high-k sites are inherently
    harder to predict, regardless of the baseline.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 3: EW |Turning Diff| (gamma = 0.206, multiplier = 1.23x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: d_turning_deg, weight_energy_predicted
Formula:
    Per sector: |d_turning_deg|
    Pair level: ew_abs_turning = SUM(w_energy_norm * |d_turning_deg|)

Description:
    Energy-weighted mean of the absolute wind deflection (turning) angle
    across all sectors.

Physical meaning:
    Large deflection angles indicate significant terrain-induced flow
    turning. WAsP's linearised BZ model handles moderate turning well
    but becomes less accurate as deflection increases, especially when
    turning exceeds 10-15 degrees.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.206 +/- 0.137
    Multiplier: 1.23x per +1 SD

Note:
    In the sector-level model, turning_gradient (rate of change between
    sectors) outperforms raw |turning|. At pair level, the energy-weighted
    mean of |turning| is used instead, since sector structure is lost
    during aggregation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 4: EW |Log Speedup Ratio| (gamma = 0.182, multiplier = 1.20x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: overall_speedup_WTG_factor, overall_speedup_MM_factor,
                weight_energy_predicted
Formula:
    Per sector: |log(speedup_WTG / speedup_MM)|
    Pair level: ew_abs_log_speedup = SUM(w_energy_norm * |log_speedup_ratio|)

Description:
    Energy-weighted mean of the absolute log-ratio of speedup factors
    between WTG and MM across all sectors.

Physical meaning:
    Large speedup differences mean the terrain affects the two locations
    very differently. A log-ratio is used because speedup varies on both
    sides of 1.0 (acceleration vs deceleration), and the log ensures
    symmetric treatment.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.182 +/- 0.126
    Multiplier: 1.20x per +1 SD

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 5: Saturating Distance (gamma = 0.159, multiplier = 1.17x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: distance_m, distance_A
Formula: dist_sat = 1 - exp(-distance_m / distance_A)

Description:
    Saturating distance function normalised by terrain-dependent lower
    acceptable distance (distance_A from FGW TR6).

    At d=0:     dist_sat = 0
    At d=dA:    dist_sat = 0.63
    At d>>dA:   dist_sat -> 1.0 (saturates)

Physical meaning:
    Wind conditions decorrelate with distance, but the relationship
    saturates — going from 10km to 15km matters less than going from
    1km to 6km. The terrain-dependent normalisation (distance_A) encodes
    that distance matters more in complex terrain.

    Unlike the clamped log(d/dA) used in the sector model, this formulation
    gives short-range pairs a non-zero (but small) contribution, with no
    hard discontinuity.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.159 +/- 0.118
    Multiplier: 1.17x per +1 SD

Note from WAsP Similarity Principle (Landberg et al. 2003):
    "The horizontal distance between the predictor and the predicted sites
    does not appear on the list [of similarity criteria]; one should much
    rather use similarity in regional wind climate."

    Distance is a PROXY for unmeasured dissimilarities, not a causal driver.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 6: Saturating |dz| (gamma = 0.105, multiplier = 1.11x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: dz
Formula: dz_sat = 1 - exp(-|dz| / 40)

Description:
    Saturating function of the absolute height difference between WTG and
    MM. Caps the effect for very large dz values.

    At |dz|=0:     dz_sat = 0
    At |dz|=40m:   dz_sat = 0.63
    At |dz|=100m:  dz_sat = 0.92 (nearly saturated)

Physical meaning:
    Height differences create different wind profiles at the two locations.
    The effect saturates because very large dz values (>80m) already
    indicate fundamentally different sites; adding more height difference
    doesn't proportionally increase uncertainty.

    The saturation addresses an observed over-prediction problem: with
    linear |dz|, pairs like Kabbo (dz=64m) were predicted at 5.1% sigma
    when actual error was only 1.8%. The saturating form caps the penalty.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.105 +/- 0.087
    Multiplier: 1.11x per +1 SD

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 7: |dz| x TRIX Interaction (gamma = 0.106, multiplier = 1.11x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: dz, TRIX_overall
Formula: dz_trix = |dz| * TRIX_overall / 100

Description:
    Height difference scaled by terrain complexity (TRIX). Large dz only
    matters when the terrain is complex — on a smooth slope, 50m of height
    difference may not cause problems.

Physical meaning:
    A 50m height difference on a gentle uniform slope (TRIX~5) means the
    two sites experience similar wind profiles at different heights — the
    log-law profile applies cleanly, and WAsP handles this well.

    The same 50m dz across a ridge (TRIX~40) means completely different
    flow regimes at the two sites — one may be in accelerated flow, the
    other in a recirculation zone. This is where dz truly drives uncertainty.

Effect:
    gamma = 0.106 +/- 0.090
    Multiplier: 1.11x per +1 SD

Note:
    This replaces the previous |dz| x dist_sat interaction. The terrain
    interaction is more physically motivated: the analysis showed that
    the old linear |dz| over-predicted for high-dz pairs in moderate
    terrain (Kabbo, Doringbaai) while under-predicting in complex terrain.


================================================================================
MU (BIAS) DRIVER
================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BIAS: Signed dz (beta_dz)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: dz
Formula: mu = beta_dz * (dz - dz_mean) / dz_std

Description:
    Signed height difference (WTG elevation - MM elevation) drives the
    expected bias direction. Positive dz (WTG higher) tends toward
    positive error (over-prediction); negative dz toward under-prediction.

Physical meaning:
    Higher elevation = higher windspeed (roughly 10% per 100m). When WTG
    is higher than MM, the cross-prediction must extrapolate upward, which
    tends to slightly over-predict. This is a systematic bias, not
    uncertainty — the model captures it in mu, not sigma.

Effect:
    beta_dz ~ 0 +/- 0.05 (modest effect, pair-specific)


================================================================================
FEATURES EXPLORED BUT NOT INCLUDED
================================================================================

The following features were tested through dedicated exploration scripts.
See Feature documentation v3 for full details.

INCLUDED PREVIOUSLY, NOW REMOVED:
    - abs_dz (linear): Replaced by dz_sat (saturating). Linear |dz|
      over-predicted for high-dz pairs in moderate terrain.
    - abs_dz_dist_sat (|dz| x distance): Replaced by dz_trix (|dz| x TRIX).
      Distance interaction was less physically motivated than terrain.
    - |dTI| (TI mismatch): Removed because TI at WTG is NEVER available at
      deployment. TI_MM could be added but was a passenger in ablation.

EXPLORED AND REJECTED:
    - Self-prediction error: Negative correlation (confounded with windspeed)
    - Roughness change count: r = -0.02 (no signal)
    - Power curve sensitivity: Negative correlation (confounded with terrain)
    - SLF / water exposure: Redundant with roughness
    - dRIX variants: Negative partial correlation (confounded with distance)
    - Turning x speedup interaction: Worse than individual components
    - Distance x RIX interaction: Already captured by dist_sat normalisation
    - Windspeed shortfall: Mean driver in signed model, not sigma driver
    - Is dominant sector: Weak signal (r = -0.108)

See Feature documentation v3, Part 3 for detailed rejection rationale.


================================================================================
KNOWN LIMITATIONS AND RESIDUAL PATTERNS
================================================================================

SYSTEMATIC OVER-PREDICTION:
    High-dz pairs in moderate terrain (TRIX < 30) are over-predicted.
    Examples: Kabbo (dz=64m, pred=3.3%, actual=1.8%), Mikolajki (dz=15m,
    pred=3.9%, actual=1.7%). The dz_sat + dz_trix features mitigate this
    vs the old linear formulation, but some over-prediction persists.

SYSTEMATIC UNDER-PREDICTION:
    Taaibos (dz=9m, dist=5.7km) is predicted at 0.5% but actual is 1.5%.
    The model sees flat, close, low roughness and assigns near-baseline sigma.
    This pair may have unmeasured sources of uncertainty (stability, mesoscale).

DIRECTIONAL ASYMMETRY:
    A->B and B->A predictions get different sigmas (due to different energy
    weights) but actual |errors| are nearly symmetric. This is a known
    artifact of energy-weighted feature aggregation. Not currently addressed.

NO PAIR RANDOM EFFECTS:
    Unlike the sector-level model, this model has no pair-specific random
    effects — with only ~28 observations, adding random effects per pair
    would overfit. New pairs at deployment get feature-driven sigma only.


================================================================================
DEPLOYMENT — WHAT YOU NEED FOR A NEW PAIR
================================================================================

PAIR-LEVEL INPUTS (one value per pair):
    distance_m      Distance between WTG and MM (metres)         GIS / map
    distance_A      FGW TR6 terrain-dependent lower bound (m)    TR6 lookup
    dz              WTG elevation - MM elevation (metres)        Elevation data
    TRIX_overall    T-RIX for the pair                           WAsP terrain

SECTOR-LEVEL INPUTS (12 values, one per sector, energy-weighted to pair):
    d_turning_deg                           Wind deflection angle        WAsP output
    overall_speedup_WTG_factor              Speedup at WTG              WAsP output
    overall_speedup_MM_factor               Speedup at MM               WAsP output
    rough_speedup_WTG_frac                  Roughness speedup at WTG    WAsP output
    rough_speedup_MM_frac                   Roughness speedup at MM     WAsP output
    k_MM                                    Weibull k at MM per sector  MM Weibull fit
    weight_energy_predicted                 Energy weight per sector    WAsP prediction

All inputs come from the predictor mast (MM) or WAsP model outputs.
Nothing requires observed data at the WTG site — deployment-ready.

The pair random effect is NOT available for new pairs; predictions use
feature-driven sigma only (baseline + 7 gamma terms).


================================================================================
CONFIDENCE INTERVAL CALCULATION
================================================================================

For a predicted sigma, calculate confidence intervals using Student-t:

    CI = +/- t_critical(nu, confidence) * sigma

For nu = 14:
    50% CI: +/- 0.692 * sigma
    68% CI: +/- 1.026 * sigma
    90% CI: +/- 1.761 * sigma
    95% CI: +/- 2.145 * sigma
    99% CI: +/- 2.977 * sigma

Example:
    If model predicts sigma = 5% for a pair:
    95% CI = +/- 2.145 * 5% = +/- 10.7%

    With mu = +1.5% (WTG higher, expect slight over-prediction):
    95% CI = [-9.2%, +12.2%]

    Interpretation: "There is 95% confidence the actual omnidirectional
    windspeed error for this pair is between -9.2% and +12.2%"


================================================================================
GLOSSARY
================================================================================

Cross-prediction:
    Predicting wind conditions at one location (WTG) using measurements
    from another location (MM), adjusted for terrain differences.

dist_sat:
    Saturating distance function: 1 - exp(-d/dA). Smooth alternative to
    the clamped distance_score. No hard cutoff at distance_A.

dz:
    Height difference between WTG and MM. Positive = WTG is higher.

Energy weight:
    Fraction of total energy production attributed to each sector.
    Based on wind frequency and windspeed (power ~ v^3).

EW (Energy-Weighted):
    Aggregation method: SUM(w_energy_norm * sector_value). Ensures
    high-energy sectors (dominant wind directions) have proportionally
    higher influence on the pair-level feature.

k (Weibull shape):
    Describes the "peakedness" of the wind speed distribution.
    k ~ 2: typical, moderate variability (Rayleigh distribution)
    k > 3: narrow speed range (channelling, trade winds)
    k < 1.5: wide speed range (variable/gusty)

MM (Measurement Mast):
    The location where wind is measured (predictor site).

Roughness speedup:
    WAsP's correction for surface roughness effects on wind speed.
    Separated from orographic (terrain shape) effects. Positive values
    indicate roughness-driven acceleration, negative = deceleration.

Sigma driver:
    A feature that increases the predicted uncertainty (sigma) when its
    value is high. All sigma drivers use HalfNormal priors (gamma >= 0),
    so they can only increase sigma, never decrease it.

TRIX:
    Terrain-based Ruggedness Index. A measure of terrain complexity used
    by FGW TR6. Higher TRIX = more complex terrain.

WAsP:
    Wind Atlas Analysis and Application Program. Industry-standard
    software for wind resource assessment based on linearised flow models.

WTG (Wind Turbine Generator):
    The location where wind conditions are being predicted. In validation,
    this is where the actual measurement exists for comparison.


